R"VG(
# medieval3.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the sermon: its sayings (the people's own proverbs; its faith's teaching and a story
# told for a lesson are medieval11.vg), the year's news as the preacher takes it up (`sermon.<kind>`, medieval5.vg and medieval6.vg),
# what to do about it. The industrial age printed its sermons and recorded them on cylinders: the same genre, its own openings.

# ---- the sermon -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.sermon (words=52..177)
  (2) {sermon.open}\n{sermon.text} {sermon.text}\n{@news public sermon}\n{sermon.turn}\n{sermon.close}
  (2) {sermon.open}\n{sermon.text}\n{@news public sermon} {@news public sermon}\n{sermon.turn} {sermon.turn}\n{sermon.close}
  (1.5) {sermon.open}\n{med.ser.story}\n{@news public sermon}\n{sermon.turn}\n{sermon.close}
  (1.5) {sermon.open}\n{med.ser.faith} {sermon.text}\n{@news public sermon}\n{med.ser.vice} {sermon.turn}\n{sermon.close}

sermon.open (formula)
  Friends, sit down, and listen; it will not take long, whatever you have heard.
  In the name of {god.who}, I speak to you at {holyplace}.
  You have come in out of {weather.now}, so listen at least until it stops.
  We are gathered at {$home} for {festival}, and I have something to say before the feast.
  Sit, all of you, even the ones at the back who think I cannot see them.
  It is {season.now} at {$home}, and the {crop} is {med.ser.cropnow}; let us think about that a while.
  (age>=industrial) A sermon preached at {holyplace}, {$home}, in {season.now}, and printed at the asking of the people.
  (age>=industrial) Friends, the bell has gone at the works, and you are tired; I will be quick.
  (faith=ancestors) Before the old ones and before you, I will say a few words.
  (faith=sky) Under {skyone}, which hears everything, I will say a little.
  (faith=land) On this ground, which is {god}'s, I will say a little.

med.ser.cropnow
  (season=0) in the ground
  (season=1) standing
  (season=2) coming in
  (season=3) in the stores
  in our hands

# the sayings: the people's proverbs and their faith's teaching
sermon.said
  It is said that
  The old ones said that
  My teacher used to say that
  They say at {holyplace} that
  There is a song that says
  Our mothers had a saying:
  You all know the saying:
  The {med.chr.trades} have a saying, and it is a good one:
  At {$home} they say
  (age>=industrial) Even in the mills they say

sermon.text
  (3) {sermon.said} {proverb}.
  {sermon.said} {proverb}. {med.ser.gloss}
  {sermon.said} {sermon.saying}
  Life, as the {med.chr.trades} of {$home} say, is {life.is}. {med.ser.gloss}
  {time.pass}, and {med.ser.still}. {med.ser.gloss}
  (faith=ancestors|faith=land) The dead are {dead.are}. {med.ser.deadnear}

med.ser.gloss (share=0.3)
  Think about that.
  (0.3) The old ones were not fools.
  My mother said it to me with {tool} in her hand.
  It was carved over the door of {holyplace} once, before the {med.ser.gone} took it.
  It is the only saying the {med.chr.trades} and the priests agree on.
  I have thought about it over the {craft} for {#n|year}, and I am not finished.
  It is truer in {season.now} than in {season.next}, but it is true in both.
  You can say it in the {crop} market at {$seat} and nobody will argue.
  Say it over the {craft}, and the {craft} goes better.
  The {med.chr.trades} of {city2} say it differently, and wrongly.
  It is older than {holyplace} and younger than {mountain}.
  Every child of {$home} has heard it over the {food} at supper.
  The old ones said it over the {herd} in the evenings, and the {herd} agreed.
  Write it on the {vessel.one.n} you drink from, if you can write.
  It was my teacher's, and her teacher's, back to the founding of {city}.
  When the {bird} come back in {season.next}, say it again.
  I have it from a {med.chr.trades.one} at {city2}, who swore it was {god}'s own.
  Some of you need it more than others; the ones by the {med.ser.door}, mostly.
  The children laugh at it, the way they laugh at the {beast}. So did I.
  Put it in your head, next to the price of {crop}.
  I say it every {festival}, and every {festival} the {drink} drowns it.
  It is cut on the lintel at {holyplace}, under {motif.over}, if you can find it.
  The {med.chr.trades} sing it at the {craft} when the master is out.
  Grandmothers in {$home} still say it to the girls who carry {watersrc.n} water.
  It is the kind of thing the old ones said over {food} and nobody wrote down.
  I learned it on {mountain}, with the {herd}, which is where most sayings come from.
  It is said in {city2} too, but there they say it about {herd}, and get the end wrong.
  When I was young I thought it was about {crop}. It is not about {crop}.

med.ser.deadnear (share=0.5)
  They are not far, and they are listening.
  They hear the {herd} come in, and they count them.
  They know the price of {crop} and they do not approve.
  At {festival} we set a bowl of {food} for them; it is still there in the morning, and that is how we know they are polite.
  They wear {colour.mourn}, I suppose, the way we wore it for them.

med.ser.door
  door
  window
  back wall
  stove

med.ser.gone
  rain
  wind
  frost
  {pest}

med.ser.still (share=0.5)
  we are still here, and {god} is still here
  the {crop} still comes up
  {holyplace} still stands, more or less
  the {herd} still need feeding
  we still quarrel about the {med.chr.quarrel.s}
  {river} still runs past the door
  the {bird} still come back in {season.next}
  we still keep {festival}, and still argue about it

sermon.saying (share=0.6)
  a house is not its walls but its fire.
  we live by {living}, and {living} lives by us.
  {skyone} keeps its time without being asked, and so should we.
  the {herd} know the way home better than the herder.
  a debt forgiven is a debt paid twice.
  the one who plants a {tree.n} does not sit in its shade.
  bread shared is heavier than bread kept.
  every year has its flood, and every flood its year.
  the stranger at the door is always somebody's child.
  a {vessel.one.n} with a crack holds water for a day and grief for a year.
  the {craft} is learned with the hands and kept with the heart.

# the vice the preacher has noticed (the people's own vices)
med.ser.vice (share=0.7)
  Put away {vice}; it is a heavier load than {crop}.
  I have seen {vice} in this town, and I have seen it in the mirror.
  We are known for {virtue}, they say at {city2}. They have not been here at {festival}, when the {drink} runs.
  {vice} has come into {$home} like {weather} under a door.
  Keep to {virtue}; it comes up slowly, like the {crop}, and lasts longer.
  {vice} is the {pest} of the soul: small, many, and hungry.
  Our mothers had {virtue} and not much else. We have much else.
  There is more {vice} in {$home} this {season.now} than {crop} in the stores.
  A little {virtue}, every day, like water on the {fruit} trees: that is all.

sermon.turn
  (2 stage<=0) So {sermon.do}, and {sermon.do}.
  (stage<=0) So {sermon.do}, and {sermon.do}, and {sermon.do}.
  (2 stage<=0) {med.ser.go}, {sermon.do}.
  (stage<=0) This {season.now}, {sermon.do}. {med.ser.small}
  (stage<=0) Before {festival}, {sermon.do}, and {sermon.do}.
  (stage<=0) {sermon.do}, and if you cannot, {sermon.do}.
  (stage>=1) {med.ser.hard}
  (stage>=2) {med.ser.end}
  (sick=1) Visit the sick{med.ser.sick}.
  (famine=1) Share what you have. {med.ser.share}
  (war=1) Pray for our own in the army, and for the others, {med.pr.mothers}.
  (temper=wry) Do better than last year. It should not be difficult.
  (temper=wry) {sermon.do}. {med.ser.wry}
  (temper=anxious) I do not know what is coming. Neither did our mothers. {sermon.do}.
  (temper=proud) We are {people}, and {med.ser.proud}. {sermon.do}, and be proud of it.
  (temper=pious) Kneel when you go in and stand when you go out; in between, {sermon.do}.

med.ser.wry (share=0.5)
  I say it every year; one year someone will.
  I have said it since my hair was {colour.any}; it is grey now, and so is the advice.
  The {herd} listen better than you do, and they are {herd}.
  I will say it again at {festival}, louder.

med.ser.go
  Go home, then
  So, this week,
  Before you sleep tonight,
  Tomorrow, before the work,
  When you get home,
  All of you, the young ones first,

med.ser.small (share=0.35)
  That is all {god} asks; it is more than it sounds.
  It is a small thing. Small things are what we have, like the {crop2} by the door.
  Nobody will thank you. {god} will notice.
  Start with that, and we will see about the rest.
  Do it before the {crop} is in, while there is time.
  The {herd} will not do it for you, and neither will I.
  You will find it harder than the {craft}, and better paid.
  It costs less than a cup of {drink} and lasts longer.
  If you forget, the {bird} on the roof will remind you; they are louder than I am.
  Your mother did it, at {$home}, in a worse year than this one, with {herd} to feed besides.

med.ser.sick
  . If you are afraid, visit them anyway
  , and take {food}, and do not stay too long
  ; the sick are lonelier than they are ill
  , and bring {drink}, and sit by the door if you must

med.ser.share
  The one who hoards bread will eat it alone.
  A full {vessel.one.n} in a hungry street is a shame, not a store.
  Your grandmother shared hers, or you would not be here.

med.ser.hard (share=0.6)
  Some of you say {god} has turned away. I say: we have turned away, and {god} is where {god} always was.
  Do not listen to the ones who say this is the end. They have said so in every year since the founding.
  Hard years make hard people. Do not let them make you hard. {sermon.do}.
  We have been here before, in the songs. The songs end well, mostly.
  The {crop} is thin and the talk is thick. Less talk, then, and more {craft}.
  Everyone is leaving for {city2}, they say. {city2} is no better; it is only further.
  Keep the lamps lit. Someone has to, and it may as well be us.

med.ser.end (share=0.6)
  Whatever comes, we will meet it here, together, at {holyplace}.
  If we must leave, we will carry {god} with us, and the names, and {med.ser.carry}.
  We are fewer every {season.any}. Sit closer.
  When the last of us goes, let the last of us shut the door gently.

med.ser.proud
  we have kept the faith longer than anyone
  our {craft} is known as far as {city2}
  we built {holyplace} with our own hands
  our mothers crossed {land} to get here

med.ser.carry
  the fire
  the {colour.holy} cloth
  the old songs
  the seed of the {crop}

sermon.do (share=0.7)
  (contact=1) be fair with the {other} in the market
  keep {festival} as it should be kept
  keep the weights of the market honest
  open your doors to the stranger
  give {god} the first cup of {drink}
  be content with the {crop} you have
  visit the old ones on the hill
  mend your neighbour's wall before your own
  teach the children the songs
  forgive the debt you were going to forgive anyway
  feed the {herd} before you feed yourselves
  sweep {holyplace} on the rest day
  say the names of the dead at {festival}
  go easy on the young; you were young
  keep {feast.custom}, and keep it properly
  {rite.imp}
  bring {food} to the house where there is sickness
  leave the last of the {crop} in the field for whoever needs it
  share {watersrc} with the late ones
  lend {tool}, and do not count the days
  put a lamp in the window for the ones on the road
  give the {craft}-house its tithe without grumbling
  stop quarrelling about the {med.chr.quarrel.s}
  wear {colour.mourn} for the dead and not a day longer

med.chr.quarrel.s
  boundary stones
  bell
  weights
  order of the procession
  {beast}

sermon.close (formula)
  Go home, and keep the lamps lit.
  That is all. {god.keep}.
  Now eat; the bread is getting cold.
  Go in peace, and go carefully; the road is bad.
  That is enough. You have work, and so have I.
  {god.keep}, and keep each other.
)VG"
